WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:04.200
Multicellular organisms begin life as a
small bundle of cells that are all

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.570
fairly similar to one another. So how
do they get from that little bundle of

3
00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:13.020
cells to a fully functional organism
that has many different kinds of cells

4
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:17.080
that perform vastly different functions?
It's through a process called cellular

5
00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.760
differentiation. It's similar to what
the name sounds like— the process of

6
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.250
making cells different from one another.
Most of the cells in that little bundle

7
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:31.000
at the beginning of life are
pluripotent. They have the potential to

8
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.910
become many different things. These are
called stem cells, and eventually they

9
00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:41.750
can become brain cells, bone cells,
blood cells and many more. Through the

10
00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:46.010
process of development, cells receive
signals that instruct them to become

11
00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:49.870
different cell types that serve
specific functions. Once cells

12
00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:53.950
differentiate into a new cell type,
they retain their new identity

13
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:54.760
permanently.

14
00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.370
They can divide into more of the same
type of cell or differentiate into

15
00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:05.260
another specialized cell type, but they
do not usually revert back to their

16
00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:09.710
beginning stem cell state. There are
ways that we can use to turn

17
00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:14.770
differentiated cells back into stem
cells. If you want to learn more, check

18
00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:20.250
out the iPSC video. In addition,
differentiation does not just occur

19
00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.360
during development of an organism. Most
adult organisms have stem cells in

20
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:29.280
their bodies that will differentiate
into specific cell types as needed. One

21
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.190
example are the stem cells located in
your bone marrow. These differentiate

22
00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:38.580
into multiple different types of blood
cells depending on what is needed. So

23
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.340
the next time you see someone who
donated blood, thank them for their

24
00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.570
generosity and thank the process of
differentiation that allows their stem

25
00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:49.250
cells to replace the blood cells that
they lost.

